  "verdict": "The raw (uncontrolled) correlation between held-out-family Mahalanobis distance and family-disjoint R2 is negative for all 5 models, consistent with the extrapolation-distance hypothesis. Distance is confounded with test_frac (partition test-set size), which independently drives apparent R2 in the multi-partition analyses (correlation with test_frac was 0.6-0.83 for several models). After controlling for test_frac by partial correlation (residualizing both distance and R2 on test_frac, then correlating residuals), the distance effect largely disappears or reverses sign for Ridge (+0.218), XGBoost (+0.017), RandomForest (+0.208) and ElasticNet (+0.498); only GBM retains a weak residual negative association (-0.108). The distance-based mechanism therefore does not explain the failure of Ridge once test-set size is controlled for (null result for this hypothesis). A more likely explanation is a model-intrinsic property: unregularized linear extrapolation produces unbounded predictions whenever any held-out family lies outside the training range on any feature dimension, a threshold-like failure mode rather than one that scales smoothly with average distance. This remains an open mechanistic question."
